    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for my diesel tech exam and i'm a bit stuck on the turbocharger part. can u explain how the exhaust gas actually turns the turbine to build boost? i get the basic idea but i need to know the specific process of how it increases the intake pressure.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A turbocharger's turbine is driven by the hot, high-velocity exhaust gases exiting the engine.  These gases, which are full of energy, are channeled into the turbine housing, causing the turbine wheel to spin at incredibly high RPMs. This spinning turbine is physically connected by a shaft to the compressor wheel, which is mounted on the other side. As the compressor spins, it sucks in the relatively low-pressure air from the intake system and forces it through the compressor blades. This action compresses the air, dramatically increasing its density and, consequently, its pressure before it enters the engine cylinders. This increased pressure is what we call \"boost.\"",
      "source": "target",
